{"commit": "136b8f1e7a4807a0662e07646b8a03c1beb7b06d", "id": "136b8f1e7a4807a0662e07646b8a03c1beb7b06d-1", "language": "rust", "reason": "Proposal names no upstream test", "repo": "https://github.com/RoaringBitmap/roaring-rs", "reproduction": {"attempt": "20260924T002448Z-9c35e4ff", "evidence_sha256": {"evidence/136b8f1e7a4807a0662e07646b8a03c1beb7b06d-1/20260924T002448Z-9c35e4ff/origin.json": "2eb3d37aef45768b37aff80b6c25b38204411039bc12607be0bc11685ae8d257", "evidence/136b8f1e7a4807a0662e07646b8a03c1beb7b06d-1/20260924T002448Z-9c35e4ff/proposal.json": "9b6a3b1cd19cd2a4a0e47180c3827e2bee6f4781008082516702bd460e0aec18"}, "provenance": "verbatim"}, "shard": "roaring-rs", "status": "blocked", "subject": "fix: bitmap advance_back_to could violate invariants", "summary": "When `BitmapIter::advance_back_to` is called with `new_key < key`, the fix returns early with a fully-reset state. The bug falls through to `(0, &mut self.value)`, writing a 0 into the front word while leaving `key_back > key` \u2014 violating the `key_back >= key` invariant and tripping `debug_assert!` on subsequent iteration.", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T00:24:48.146508+00:00"}
